STREAMING TRANSACTION RECONCILIATION ENGINE

Project Overview

This project modernizes a legacy banking operations dashboard into a
real-time Streaming Transaction Reconciliation Engine using Angular
Standalone architecture.

The system simulates high-frequency financial transactions (ACH, WIRE,
CARD), implements optimistic UI updates, interceptor-based
reconciliation, defensive programming patterns, and observability
mechanisms.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Architecture Decisions

-   Angular Standalone Components
-   Signal-based Global Store (Reactive State Management)
-   OnPush Change Detection Strategy
-   Functional HTTP Interceptors
-   Web Worker for heavy aggregation
-   Feature-based modular folder structure
-   Role-Based Access Control simulation

------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Core Features Implemented

Phase 1 – Signal-Based Store - Created TransactionStore using Angular
signals - Maintains immutable transaction state - Provides computed
summaries (ACH/WIRE/CARD breakdown) - Logs state changes

Phase 2 – Streaming Engine - Simulated 50+ transactions per second using
RxJS interval - Random transaction generator - Real-time UI updates via
signals

Phase 3 – Optimistic UI & Rollback - Transactions immediately appear as
PENDING - HTTP Interceptor simulates 3-second server reconciliation - On
success → marked SUCCESS - On failure → rolled back to FAILED

Phase 4 – Idempotency Layer - Prevents duplicate submissions - Uses Set
to track processing transaction IDs - Defensive programming against
double-clicks

Phase 5 – Web Worker Aggregation - Heavy summary calculations moved to
aggregation.worker.ts - Keeps main UI thread responsive at high
throughput - Updates summary metrics asynchronously

Phase 6 – Observability (DSP) - Developer Streaming Panel (DSP) -
Displays throughput (tx/sec) - Shows transaction logs - Monitors system
behavior in real time

Phase 7 – HTTP Interceptor Reconciliation - Functional interceptor
intercepts ‘/api/reconcile’ - Simulates network latency (3 seconds) -
Random success/failure simulation - Separates reconciliation logic from
UI layer

